If you've been listening in on the past several podcasts, we've had a few episodes where we get to talk with two of my old Eagle Scouts who are out through hiking the Appalachian Trail.

106
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,000
It's been great fun.

107
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:11,000
They are in town this week. They're coming to the troop meeting.

108
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:16,000
I'm going to get to talk with them and we'll record something for an upcoming podcast.

109
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:22,000
So anyway, you've sent me email questions. I've got answers, So let's get started. shall we

110
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:50,000
Write me a letter? Send it by name

111
00:07:50,000 --> 00:08:04,000
Email, that is, folks. And here's an answer to one of your emails.

112
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:12,000
Steve Berthium, who is the scout master of troop 46 in thingsboro, got in touch to say this:

113
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:18,000
This issue appears to have no easy answer. I have a second class scout who hates camping.

114
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:28,000
He's popular with his peers, He helps out on service project, He intends meetings, but he's just not going to go camping overnight, even if a parent comes along.

115
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:35,000
His parents are a loss to explain it. There's certainly not overbearing helicopter parents that one might associate with this type of issue.

116
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:41,000
Camping just seems so central to the benefits of scouting. Can a boy succeed in the program without it?

117
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:45,000
Well, Steve, there's no question that camping is scouting and vice versa.

118
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:52,000
And this scout that you're talking about, if he's second class, he's had to have gone camping overnight and absolutely refusing to go.

119
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:59,000
I think that something may have gone on on an earlier trip. So my first step would be to ask him about this.

120
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:04,000
Steve got back to me and said I spoke with the scout. I told him I wanted to make sure I hadn't missed anything,

121
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:09,000
He hadn't had any bad experiences. He said none of that had happened. He just hates going camping.

122
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:16,000
He also told me he planned to leave the troops soon And I asked him to try and stay at least until we reach charter. but he was pretty noncommittal.

123
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:22,000
Well, Steve, I guess when I was nine or 10, maybe maybe a little younger- I decided I wanted to play a little league baseball.

124
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:27,000
Actually, I had no idea what I was getting myself into, because I was nine or 10..

125
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:33,000
I was, you know my friends were playing baseball. I thought, well, that's what you do, So let's go play baseball.

126
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:37,000
And I found the whole thing kind of confusing and really, really boring. frankly,

127
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:43,000
I grew to not like it very much at all My parents insisted that I see that season out

128
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:49,000
And actually when it came down for the next season, even though I hadn't really liked it very much, I signed up again.

129
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:53,000
I went to one or two practices and I realized I wanted nothing to do with it.

130
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:59,000
So I stopped. I quit Now. I suppose I could have gotten a lot out of the experience, but around 50 years later

131
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:04,000
I don't regret not having played baseball or any other sport. to tell you the truth,

132
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:08,000
I was just kind of as out of place on a sports team as one could possibly be

133
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:12,000
And I suppose I would still be kind of out of place. I don't hate sports,

134
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:15,000
It's just something I don't even have the most passing interest in.

135
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:18,000
It's a totally inaccessible experience for me.

136
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:24,000
When it comes down to things like this, maybe you know, sometimes boys have like two settings:

137
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:29,000
They love something or they hate something. That's their binary,

138
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:35,000
That's the way they express their feelings. I don't think that the scout you're talking about hates camping.

139
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:39,000
It's maybe just not something he wants to have anything to do with.

140
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:43,000
He might not find the experience to be accessible to him at all-

141
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
And I get that. I don't have a problem with that-

142
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:50,000
And absent, his parents resolve to require this of him,

143
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:54,000
He'll probably stop being a scout, because scouting means going camping.

144
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:59,000
He doesn't really think camping is all that great and he's not interested in doing it.

145
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:02,000
And I again, I don't have a problem with that

146
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:06,000
And I have had scouts in the past. you know, they spend a couple of years in the troop.

147
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:09,000
They may even advance a little bit and then they're at a point where they go.

148
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:13,000
you know this, I'm not this. this isn't working for me.

149
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:16,000
I have other things I want to do, better ways to spend my time,

150
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:20,000
And you know what? That's fine. I didn't make a very good baseball player.

151
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:23,000
Some boys just aren't interested in doing scouts.

152
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:27,000
I think that your due diligence is to find out what's going on,

153
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:30,000
make sure that there's not a problem that can be fixed.

154
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:36,000
But in the end there is a percentage of boys who are not going to be interested in being scouts.

155
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:40,000
And scouting casts a pretty broad net, but it can't be everything to everybody.

156
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:45,000
It has a focus and a concentration and some people are going to like it and some people aren't.

157
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
So I don't have a problem with that.

158
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:50,000
I know it's never good news when this happens, but it's nobody's fault.

159
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:56,000
If indeed this scout does leave the troop, make sure you assure him you aren't distressed or disappointed in him

160
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:59,000
and wish him well and tell him the door is always open if he changes his mind.

161
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:03,000
The sender of this next email asked to remain anonymous.

162
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:09,000
They said: can a scout master and assistant scout master make a scout sign a leadership contract

163
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:13,000
that they will abide by all the rules, be active, serve entire term of leadership

164
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:16,000
and attend most of the troop meetings and troop outing?

165
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:21,000
And if they can actually do this, shouldn't the scout master request approval from the adult committee

166
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
before taking the contract to the scouts?

167
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:27,000
Well, let's break this down into three questions,

168
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:30,000
Two questions you asked me, and both are pretty easy to answer.

169
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:35,000
Can a scout master or assistant scout master make a scout sign a leadership contract?

170
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:36,000
I suppose so.

171
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:42,000
Yes, I don't see anything that prevents that, but we'll discuss more about that in a moment.

172
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:47,000
The second question is: shouldn't the scout master request approval from the committee

173
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
before taking a contract to the scouts?

174
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:55,000
You know, I think it would be a good idea, but there's no real statements of policy

175
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:58,000
that I can find describing that kind of approval.

176
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:03,000
Hopefully everybody knows what's going on, because the scout master is discussing things with the committee

177
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:08,000
and the committee is discussing things with the scout master, and there's a dialogue going on there all the time

178
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:09,000
and this would come up.

179
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:13,000
The third question is the one that I ask, and maybe it's the most important one,

180
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:22,000
is that should a scout master or assistant scout master make a scout sign a leadership contract?

181
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:28,000
Well, if you're asking me- and you are- I would say no,

182
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:33,000
And I say this advisedly, because a couple of decades ago I thought that this was a great idea,

183
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:36,000
but it wasn't. It's a bad idea,

184
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:42,000
I guess. at the time I thought that a contract would give me some leverage

185
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:48,000
or it would teach scouts an appreciation of the kind of responsibility they were expected to take on.

186
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:53,000
And we printed that stupid contract out and, of course, they signed it.

187
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:58,000
but they didn't behave any differently than scouts who had never had a contract.

188
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:01,000
They missed meetings and trips, just like before.

189
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:05,000
I don't know what I was going to do with that contract.

190
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:08,000
I mean, what am I going to do? Take them to court,

191
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:13,000
You know? or I guess the best thing I could do with it is show them a piece of paper

192
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:17,000
with their signature on it and say: well, you haven't been keeping up with your contract.

193
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:21,000
Don't you feel horrible? So I could make scouts feel bad for being teenagers,

194
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
and that's kind of shooting fish in a barrel.

195
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:27,000
There are plenty of other people who do that, so why not just join in?

196
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:33,000
But you know, what I came to realize is this: Scouts are volunteering their time just like I am.

197
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:37,000
I don't have to sign a contract, and there's something about being taken at one's word.

198
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:41,000
I am not their employer. I'm not their boss or their drill sergeant.

199
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:46,000
I am only there to make an opportunity possible for them.

200
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:51,000
And anyone working with young people is going to need to accept that. they're young, inexperienced and barometric.

201
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:55,000
They're going to make plenty of mistakes. They've got giant blind spots.

202
00:14:55,000 --> 00:15:00,000
They have no experience. You know they're going to come into something like this

203
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:04,000
not understanding at all what they're getting themselves into.

204
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:08,000
They're going to have a problem comprehending what their responsibility is.

205
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:12,000
But with a little bit of work you learn how to be encouraging

206
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:16,000
and how to tap that vast potential that they all have

207
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:20,000
and help them develop themselves into good, contributing adults.

208
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:23,000
And you can't really accomplish that with a contract.

209
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:28,000
What you can accomplish it with is having an open dialogue with your Scouts

210
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:31,000
If you're frustrated about something or if they're missing something

211
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:36,000
or if they don't understand something you know. that's why you're there:

212
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:39,000
To coach them along, to encourage them.

213
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:42,000
Every tool in our toolbox is a positive thing.

214
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:45,000
If you're using negative methods, get rid of them.

215
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:48,000
Stay positive, Be encouraging, Be helpful,

216
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:54,000
But most of all, understand that you cannot achieve what you think you're going to achieve

217
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:57,000
using something like a contract. Come on

218
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:00,000
Now. the person who originally sent me that question says:

219
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,000
I really appreciate your response.

220
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:06,000
We had a couple of Scouts who didn't perform very well in their positions of responsibility

221
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
and they were fired.

222
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:11,000
I just thought a Scout couldn't be relieved of their leadership.

223
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
Thanks so much for your advice. I'm very grateful.

224
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:16,000
You know I want to differentiate between a couple of things

225
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:19,000
of what a Scout Master can do within the policies and procedures,

226
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:24,000
as I understand them, and the attitude that's the basis of their actions.

227
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:29,000
So the actions that you describe like insisting on that Scouts sign a contract

228
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:33,000
or firing them because they're not doing their job well,

229
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:37,000
is not specifically prohibited in any policies procedures I know of.

230
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:41,000
Once again, as the program is described, it's described in positives, not negatives,

231
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:46,000
In the good things that you can do, not the bad things that you shouldn't do.

232
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:49,000
And when people are doing something like this, it be lies

233
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:53,000
and a lack of understanding on their part of what Scouting is

234
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:56,000
and a problematic attitude towards young people.

235
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:59,000
And when a Scout Master adopts this kind of problematic attitude,

236
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:02,000
my general advice is leaving them to themselves

237
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:06,000
and finding another troop where the Scout Master better understands their role.

238
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:10,000
I mean, sometimes people say they want to change what's going on

239
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
and I tell them not to waste their time

240
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:15,000
If it's possible to find another troop that gets Scouting

241
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
and doesn't deal with these sorts of things.

242
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:21,000
I heard from Mike Rosslender who's the Scout Master, Troop 208

243
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:24,000
in Westlake, Ohio, and he said

244
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:28,000
I was listening to podcast 199,. wow, we're going back a ways

245
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:31,000
about the rule in the Guide to Advancement.

246
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:36,000
The words of review should be composed of registered committee members.

247
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:39,000
There's some exceptions for smaller units,

248
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:42,000
but the bias towards registration is high.

249
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
Registration of adults costs money.

250
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:47,000
I think that they are already donating their time

251
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
and we ought not to be asking them to pay for the privilege.

252
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:53,000
Why would any rational committee chair register more than a minimum

253
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:56,000
of adults during annual rechartering? anyway?

254
00:17:56,000 --> 00:18:00,000
I know as a cub leader I didn't really understand the Boy Scout program

255
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:03,000
until I sat on my first board of review.

256
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:07,000
I think once parents see how the program really works,

257
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:10,000
they stop fighting it and better support their sons.

258
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:13,000
So I don't really understand why we can't have parents

259
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
sitting on a board of review

260
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:18,000
and it's limited only to registered committee members.

261
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
What am I missing?

262
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:24,000
Well, Mike, I think, as laudable as the goal is there.

263
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:27,000
I think what you're missing is registered adults

264
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:30,000
have to have a current youth protection training certificate.

265
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:33,000
They also have to pass a background check,

266
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,000
And I believe that's an excellent rationale

267
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:38,000
for limiting the participation in boards of review

268
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
to registered adult volunteers.

269
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:43,000
Unregistered parents on the board,

270
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:45,000
even if it's not for their own son,

271
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:48,000
can possibly be seen as being too difficult

272
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:51,000
or going too easy on somebody else's son

273
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:55,000
If they're not practiced in the way that boards go.

274
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,000
that makes every board a training experience,

275
00:18:57,000 --> 00:19:00,000
which I don't know that I particularly care for that idea,

276
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:04,000
But I do understand that listening to what goes on

277
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:07,000
at a board of review could be very informative.

278
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:10,000
However, I didn't make the rules.

279
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
The BSA makes the rules

280
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:15,000
and we need a minimum of three adults from the committee

281
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
to conduct a board of review,

282
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,000
And I know that that can be a challenge sometimes.

283
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
I'd recommend this to you

284
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
Have a regularly scheduled board of review.

285
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
We do one once a month.

286
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,000
Plan ahead, and you know

287
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:29,000
you have so many committee members.

288
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,000
Get them on the roster,

289
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:32,000
Get them to do it.

290
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
And when it comes to the idea that, yes,

291
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:38,000
it costs money for adults to register as volunteers-

292
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:39,000
it's a pain,

293
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
You know. they have to go through training.

294
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:44,000
That's a pain, everything like that-

295
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:48,000
Well, sometimes we just have to settle for the idea

296
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
that there is a price of doing business.

297
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
You're going to be a volunteer scouter.

298
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
That means you are accepting the idea

299
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
that it's going to cost you a little bit of money.

300
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
It's going to cost you some training, time

301
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:01,000
and things that perhaps you'd rather not spend.

302
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:04,000
But in my experience

303
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:07,000
the BSA has the lowest bar

304
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:10,000
for becoming a registered volunteer

305
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
of any other scouting organization

306
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
in the rest of the world.

307
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:17,000
I am an eternal foe of things like paperwork

308
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,000
and filling out forms and paying fees.

309
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:22,000
Nobody likes it, but it's a price of doing business

310
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:25,000
And so we just it's something that we deal with.

311
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,000
So, Mike, I hope that helps.

